WEBVTT

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:02.240
<v NARRATOR>Your immune system is brilliant.

00:00:02.260 --> 00:00:04.860
But the first time it meets a new germ,
it's slow.

00:00:06.385 --> 00:00:10.165
<v NARRATOR>Building the right defence can
take a week or more. And a week is a long

00:00:10.165 --> 00:00:10.985
time to be sick.

00:00:12.310 --> 00:00:16.390
<v NARRATOR>A vaccine is a rehearsal. It
shows your body a harmless piece of the

00:00:16.390 --> 00:00:20.050
germ, or instructions to make one, so it
can practise without the danger.

00:00:21.508 --> 00:00:24.788
<v NARRATOR>Your body answers with
antibodies, and with memory cells that

00:00:24.788 --> 00:00:26.848
keep the germ's shape on file, often for
years.

00:00:28.408 --> 00:00:32.428
<v NARRATOR>So when the real germ arrives,
those memory cells know it at once. A

00:00:32.428 --> 00:00:34.208
defence that once took a week now starts

00:00:34.208 --> 00:00:36.628
within days, often before you feel ill at
all.

00:00:38.044 --> 00:00:40.344
<v STUDENT>Wait. So the vaccine doesn't
fight the germ itself?

00:00:41.829 --> 00:00:43.949
<v NARRATOR>Exactly. It trains the team that
does.

00:00:45.454 --> 00:00:48.814
<v NARRATOR>And when enough people are
protected, a germ struggles to spread at

00:00:48.814 --> 00:00:52.554
all. That shields the people who can't be
vaccinated, like newborn babies.
